---
// source: b433a07857b6
import CaseLink from '../../components/CaseLink.astro';
import { euroRange, itemRange, objective, severanceMonths } from '../../content/examples';
import { euros } from '../../content/format';
import type { Lang } from '../../i18n/languages';

interface Props {
  lang: Lang;
}
const { lang } = Astro.props;

const severance = itemRange(objective, 'severance');
const notice = itemRange(objective, 'employer_notice');
const salary = itemRange(objective, 'pending_salary');
const holidays = itemRange(objective, 'holiday_pay');
const extraPay = itemRange(objective, 'extra_pay');
const unfair = objective.unfairReference;
const months =
  severanceMonths(
    objective.items.find((p) => p.item.id === 'severance')?.item.calculation ?? [],
  )[0] ?? 0;
if (!severance || !notice || !salary || !holidays || !extraPay || unfair === null)
  throw new Error('Incomplete example');
---

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="do-20-dias">20 days per year, with a cap of 12 monthly payments</h3>
  <p>
    In a dismissal on objective grounds (despido objetivo), the employer has to make available to
    you, together with the letter, severance pay of 20 days’ pay per year worked, up to a maximum of
    12 monthly payments (art. 53.1.b ET). The CGPJ guide, following STS 651/2026, counts that cap as
    360 days. Time is counted in months, and part of a month counts as a whole month.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>53.1.b</span> ET
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="do-preaviso">15 days’ notice, or payment in lieu</h3>
  <p>
    Between the letter and the dismissal there have to be 15 days’ notice (art. 53.1.c ET). The days
    the employer does not give are paid. The calculator asks you how many you were given and
    calculates the ones missing.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>53.1.c</span> ET
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="do-ejemplo">Worked example</h3>
  <p>
    A person earning 1.500&nbsp;€ gross a month with two extra payments of 1.500&nbsp;€ works from 3
    May 2018 to 20 July 2026. They have taken 10 days of holiday this year and the employer gives
    them no notice. These are the calculator’s figures:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Severance pay: {euroRange(severance, lang)} ({months} months × 20 / 12 ={' '}
      {String((months * 20) / 12).replace('.', ',')} days).
    </li>
    <li>Notice not given: {euroRange(notice, lang)}.</li>
    <li>July pay: {euroRange(salary, lang)}.</li>
    <li>Untaken holiday: {euroRange(holidays, lang)}.</li>
    <li>Christmas extra payment accrued since 1 July: {euroRange(extraPay, lang)}.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="example">
    <span class="example__label">If it were declared unfair</span> With the same dates, the
    severance pay would be {euros(unfair)}, at 33 days per year. The review shows you that figure
    beside the one for the objective-grounds dismissal.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="do-diferencia">Objective-grounds or unfair dismissal</h3>
  <p>
    An objective-grounds dismissal can be challenged in the same way as a disciplinary one (art.
    53.3 ET). If it is declared unfair, the severance pay is calculated at 33 days per year, with a
    cap of 720 days: you will find it in <CaseLink lang={lang} id="unfair_dismissal" />. The time
    limit to challenge it is 20 business days (días hábiles) (art. 59.3 ET).
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>53 · 56 · 59</span> ET
  </p>
</section>
